{
  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "All Other",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed development fails to demonstrate&nbsp;that additional demand for parking from the development could be adequately accommodated in the surrounding area. The proposal is therefore likely to add unacceptably to parking stress and congestion in the vicinity of the application site.&nbsp; As such, it fails to comply with the following policies&nbsp;which seek to ensure proposals show how car parking will be designed and managed and to take into consideration on-street parking availability and existing and planned public transport provision: National Planning Policy Framework (December 2024) Policies T1, T6 and T6.1 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies DMT1 and&nbsp;DMT2 of the Barking and Dagenham Local Plan (September 2024)",
    "The proposal fails to demonstrate that the HMO would have capacity for up to 10 residents. The proposal would only consist of 4 bedrooms, creating a cramped living environment,&nbsp;creating a sub-standard quality of accomodation, causing harm to the living standards of future occupiers.&nbsp;The proposal is therefore considered to be at odds with the following policies:DCLG Technical Housing Standards (nationally described space standard) (DCLG, March 2015) (as amended)Policy D6 and Table 3.1 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policy DMD 1 of the London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Local Plan (September 2024)",
    "The proposed development represents incremental intensification of the site. Due to the significant increase in occupancy, the proposal would result in a harmful loss of residential amenity, increasing comings and goings, noise and general disturbance for neighbouring occupiers.&nbsp;National Planning Policy Framework (December 2024)Policies&nbsp;GG3, D6 and D14 of the London Plan (March 2021)",
    "The proposal lacks clarity regarding the internal alterations proposed.&nbsp; Particularly, the Proposed Floor Plans are the same as the Existing Floor Plans. The plans therefore fail to demonstarte any internal changes or how the HMO would have capacity for the additional residents.&nbsp;The proposal could prejudice third parties who may otherwise have wished to comment on the application; the proposed development cannot be accurately assessed and it cannot be demonstrated that it would be supported by the Development Plan.National Planning Policy Framewor (NPPF) (MHCLG, December 2024)Policy D4 of the London Plan (March 2021);Policies SP2 and&nbsp;DMD1 of&nbsp;The London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Local Plan (September 2024)"
  ],
